<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Etemenanki</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Etemenanki, 'temple of the foundation of heaven and earth', was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, standing beside his temple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esagila">Esagila</a>. Rebuilt on a vast scale by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, it rose in stepped stages over a base about 91 metres square. It is widely thought to have inspired the biblical story of the Tower of Babel and was dismantled after the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/etemenanki.jpg" alt="Etemenanki" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jona Lendering at en.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etemenanki_drawing.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
